 Use `scripts/replay_metrics.py RUN_DIR` to validate a completed local record and create one fresh Trackio run. It never appends automatically to an existing Trackio run, so a retry creates another explicitly identifiable projection rather than risking duplicate observations inside the original. Original step values and event order are replayed, but Trackio's public `log(metrics, step=...)` API does not accept the source timestamp; wall-clock timestamps therefore become replay time. Context fields are retained in the authoritative JSONL but are not automatically converted into Trackio metrics.
 
 Trackio 0.35 does not expose a durable per-event acknowledgement through this logging API. A monotonic source sequence is useful for validation, but does not provide exactly-once ingestion by itself. The bundled replay reports warnings and an uncertain result instead of claiming an acknowledgement or cursor guarantee that the destination did not provide.
+
+## Installed Lens recorder
+
+When the user authorized Trackio recording and Lens is installed, its maintained
+`trackio-lens record` command accepts this schema as `--events metrics.jsonl`
+and a manifest with `run_id`, `project`, `name`, and optional `config`. Use
+`--report NEW_PRIVATE_FILE` to retain one attempt. The command discovers the
+installed local destination; an explicit `--data-dir` chooses another destination.
+The producer remains plain C++17; the recorder uses the Lens installation’s
+isolated Python and official SDK. It needs no C++/Python/Rust ABI bridge or HTTP.
+
+Offline import validates the complete stream first. Optional `--follow` consumes
+complete lines; `--done-file FILE` signals producer termination, not workload
+success. Preserve the producer’s own exit status. Each invocation creates a fresh
+projection; failed attempts are never silently resumed. Check destination readback
+and the result report, retain original timestamps in JSONL, and do not interpret
+progress counters as exactly-once acknowledgements. The installed repository’s
+`docs/recording.md` owns the recorder interface and examples.
